== 2.1 Functions and Divisions

The skeleton supports the body, protects organs and provides a framework for movement with muscles and joints. Bone also stores minerals, and bone marrow is involved in blood-cell formation. The skull protects the brain; the rib cage helps protect the heart and lungs. Bones work together with other structures rather than moving by themselves.

The *axial skeleton* includes the skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum. The *appendicular skeleton* includes the limbs and the shoulder and pelvic girdles that connect them to the axial framework. The femur is the thigh bone; the humerus is in the upper arm. The tibia and fibula are in the lower leg; the radius and ulna are in the forearm.

#rule-box("Identify the location first")[
  *Femur* belongs to the thigh; *humerus* belongs to the upper arm. A name that sounds familiar is not enough: locate the bone before choosing an option.
]

== 2.2 Connective Structures

*Cartilage* is a supportive tissue found in several places, including on the ends of bones at many movable joints, where it helps reduce friction and distribute load. A *ligament* connects bone to bone and helps stabilise a joint. A *tendon* connects muscle to bone and transmits force from the muscle to the skeleton.

A *joint* is a place where bones meet. The amount and type of movement vary with structure. A synovial joint has a joint cavity and structures that support movement; synovial fluid helps lubricate the joint. Do not assume that all joints move freely: the skull includes joints with very limited movement.

