== 2.3 Joint Types and Movements

A *hinge* joint mainly permits flexion and extension: the elbow is a common example. A *ball-and-socket* joint permits movement in several directions; the shoulder and hip are examples. The shoulder and hip are not identical in their individual ranges or stability. A *pivot* joint permits rotation around an axis; rotation between the first two cervical vertebrae is an example.

*Flexion* generally decreases the angle between body segments; *extension* generally increases it. *Abduction* moves a limb away from the body's midline, while *adduction* brings it towards the midline. These introductory descriptions depend on the joint and reference position used.

#concept-box("The direction pair")[
  *Abduction* is away from the midline; *adduction* is towards it. At the elbow, bending is flexion and straightening is extension.
]

== 2.4 Posture and Care

*Posture* describes how the body is positioned or aligned. It changes with activity and is not a single rigid pose that everyone must maintain at all times. Comfortable positioning, varied movement, suitable equipment and appropriately progressed activity can support participation. Persistent or worsening pain, injury, weakness or other concerning symptoms warrant assessment by a qualified health professional.

Some spinal curves are normal. Terms such as *scoliosis*, *kyphosis* and *lordosis* describe different patterns or curvatures, but a label from a diagram is not a diagnosis. Causes, severity and appropriate management vary; assessment and treatment should be individualised.

== 2.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Locate the bone", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [The femur is found in the \
A. thigh \
B. upper arm \
C. forearm \
D. skull],
  [The femur is the thigh bone. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Connect bone to bone", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A structure that connects bone to bone and helps stabilise a joint is a \
A. tendon \
B. ligament \
C. muscle fibre \
D. nerve impulse],
  [A ligament connects bones; a tendon connects muscle to bone. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Movement at the elbow", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [Bending the elbow is principally an example of \
A. adduction of the shoulder \
B. rotation of the neck \
C. flexion \
D. extension],
  [Bending generally decreases the angle at the elbow, which is flexion. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "The joint and its range", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [Which pairing is most appropriate? \
A. Elbow — ball-and-socket \
B. Hip — immovable skull joint \
C. Shoulder — hinge only \
D. Hip — ball-and-socket],
  [The hip is a ball-and-socket joint. The elbow is commonly described as a hinge joint. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Read the structure and the movement")[
  Ligament: bone to bone. Tendon: muscle to bone. Flexion: generally decreases an angle. Abduction: away from the midline. Examples are simplified; actual anatomy and movement can be more complex.
]

